@startuml dataflow_example_multi_var
' Worked example: notebooks/tutorial3.ipynb, case "overlap_le1_1d".
' Two variables on one 3x3 grid, the second varying along one dimension.
' Every number here is from an actual run of that case.
' Render: plantuml docs/dataflow_example_multi_var.puml

title Example 2 -- two variables, one of them 1D (tutorial3, "overlap_le1_1d")

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FCFCFC
  BorderColor #777777
  DiamondBackgroundColor #F2F2F2
}
skinparam partition {
  BorderColor #8899AA
  FontColor #33495E
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFDF0
  BorderColor #C8C08A
}

start

partition "call" {
  :GenerateData(num_obs=8, num_dims=2, ratio_dims=1,
                density=[8/9, 8/9], seed=12345,
                num_vars=2, var_dims=[2, 1],
                overlap=1/8, fixed_overlap=False)
  .generate(...);
  note right
    var_dims=[2,1]: var0 varies along both dimensions,
    var1 along one. The GRID stays 2D either way.
    overlap is F1 -- the share of <b>var0's</b> sites that
    also carry var1, on the dimensions the two share.
  end note
}

partition "validate and configure" {
  :shape = [3, 3], 9 sites, density_zero = 3/9 = 0.333;

  :<b>MultiVarDimensionsConfig</b>  streams VAR_DIMS, CONST_COORD
  var_dims_indices: var0 varies along dimensions 0 and 1,
    var1 along dimension 1 only
  var_constant_dims: none for var0, dimension 0 for var1
  so var1 is pinned to <b>x0 index 2</b> and varies along x1;

  :<b>MultiVarOverlapConfig</b>
  var1 varies along one axis of length 3, so it has 3 sites,
  not 8. Its count is set from the overlap instead:
  round(1/8 * 8) = 1
  -> var_num_obs = <b>[8, 1]</b>, var_densities = [0.889, 0.889];
  note right #FDF1F1
    printed: "Variable 1 requested 8 observations but
    only has 3 grid points (varying dims: [1]).
    Reducing to 1 observations based on overlap 0.1250."
  end note

  :<b>dim_split = 1</b>, not 0
  the split axis must be one EVERY variable varies along,
  and var1 is constant along x0;
  note right
    printed: "Stratifying along dimension 1 (size 3)
    rather than the largest dimension 0 (size 3),
    which is not shared by every variable."
  end note
}

partition "coordinates" {
  :the same two streams as the single-variable case, so the
  axes depend on the seed and the shape and nothing else:
  x0 = [0.1809, 0.3500, 0.6837],
  x1 = [0.7887, 0.9100, 0.9353];
}

partition "placement: var0 first, then var1 per stratum" {
  :<b>var0</b>: the single-variable routine, split along x1
  LHS (3 points) + apportion(8 - 3 = 5) over the 3 strata
  -> fill counts <b>[1, 2, 2]</b>, drawn with stream(12345, STRATUM, j);
  note right
    <b>var0 record</b> (. = vacant)
             x1=0    x1=1    x1=2
      x0=0   0.320   0.742   0.134
      x0=1     .     0.802   0.547
      x0=2   0.550   0.023   0.868
    8 of 9 sites.
  end note

  :<b>var1</b>: it varies along the split axis only, so its
  shared-dimension plane is a single cell per stratum
  apportion(1 observation, weights [1,1,1]) -> <b>[0, 0, 1]</b>
  var1 exists in stratum x1=2 alone;

  :in that stratum: proj(S0) = 1 cell, free cells = 0
  ideal = round(0.125 * 1) = 0, but lo = 1 (nowhere else to go)
  -> n_overlap = 1, clipped, drawn with stream(12345, VAR, 1, 2);
  note right #FDF1F1
    printed: "WARNING var1: overlap target not reachable
    in 1 of 3 strata (e.g. wanted 0 of 1 reference cells,
    used 1; 0 cells free of var0). Density takes precedence."
    A 1D variable on a saturated reference cannot hit an
    arbitrary overlap: the pair (density, overlap) is
    over-determined. See docs/explainer_multivar.md.
  end note

  :<b>OverlapCalculator.compute_overlap_report</b>
  on x1, var0 uses all 3 coordinates and var1 uses 1 of them
  <b>F1 = 1/3</b> (target was 0.125), <b>F2 = 1.000</b>;
  note right
    var1 record, full grid (. = vacant)
             x1=0    x1=1    x1=2
      x0=0     .       .       .
      x0=1     .       .       .
      x0=2     .       .     0.759
    One value, at the pinned x0 index 2.
  end note
}

partition "two in-memory stores, which differ in shape here" {
  :<b>NetCDFBuilder.build_dataset</b>
  var0 (x0, x1) 3x3, then squeeze_constant_dims drops x0
  from var1, leaving <b>var1 (x1)</b> = NaN, NaN, 0.759
  attrs add num_vars=2, var_densities, var_num_obs=[8,1],
  overlap_target=[0.125], overlap_convention,
  fixed_overlap=[0], overlap_actual_f1=[0.333], _f2=[1.0];
  note right
    Squeezing is deliberate: a variable pinned to one
    coordinate carries no information along that axis,
    and a NaN slab would inflate the array side, which
    is the thing being measured.
    It does cost var1's x0 label -- the netCDF no longer
    says which x0 the value sat at; the parquet does.
  end note

  :<b>ParquetBuilder.build_multi_var_dataframe</b>
  one row per OCCUPIED coordinate (8 of them, the union),
  one column per variable, x1 (= dim_split) slowest;
  note right
    |= x0 |= x1 |= var0 |= var1 |
    | 0.180929 | 0.788706 | 0.320146 | NaN |
    | 0.683725 | 0.788706 | 0.550290 | NaN |
    | 0.180929 | 0.910005 | 0.742040 | NaN |
    | 0.349984 | 0.910005 | 0.802223 | NaN |
    | 0.683725 | 0.910005 | 0.023239 | NaN |
    | 0.180929 | 0.935274 | 0.134245 | NaN |
    | 0.349984 | 0.935274 | 0.547495 | NaN |
    | 0.683725 | 0.935274 | 0.868300 | 0.75896 |
    A variable absent at a coordinate costs one NaN cell
    per row here, and a whole grid slot on the array side.
  end note
}

partition "disk" {
  :ds.nc                       13243 B
  parquet/ddf_0.parquet         3871 B
  parquet/_metadata             3326 B
  parquet/_common_metadata      2704 B;
}

:return (Dataset, DataFrame);
stop

@enduml
